7. HOW THE ALLOWANCE IS MEASURED, AND THE MARK ON FREE OUTPUT

7.1  The Software keeps a usage record on your own computer counting the
     images and the volume of video it has processed. When a free allowance in
     clause 2.1 is reached, the Software stops processing that kind of file and
     tells you how to obtain a licence. This record stays on your machine.

7.2  Licence keys are verified on your machine using a public key built into
     the Software. Activation works offline.

7.3  The Software does not contact us. It has no telemetry, sends no usage
     reports, and transmits nothing about you, your files or your machine. The
     only network access it makes at all is downloading model weights on first
     use, from a public host.

7.4  Output produced under the Free Tier carries an invisible, machine-readable
     mark identifying the tier, the installation, and the position in your
     allowance at the time the file was made. The mark is designed not to be
     perceptible, and does not alter what the image depicts.

7.5  We disclose the mark here, in the interface and in the documentation
     because you are entitled to know what is in files you produce. It
     identifies the Software and the licence tier. It does not contain your
     name, your files, your location or any personal information, and it is
     never transmitted anywhere: it exists only in the files you keep, and can
     only be read by someone you give a file to.

7.6  Output produced under a Personal or Commercial Licence carries no mark.

7.7  You may satisfy yourself about clauses 7.3 to 7.6 by reading the source
     code, which is published in full.

7.8  Removing or defeating the usage record, the licence check or the mark in
     order to use the Software beyond your allowance is a breach of clause
     5.2(a) and of this agreement.


